title: Version scope and non-goals
tags: Documentation
kind: rationale
aliases: non-goals scope roadmap deferred
summary: The documentation describes version 0.3, all of it implemented unless a note says otherwise; no remote protocol, no user accounts, no two daemons on one repository.

!! What the documentation describes

Version ''0.3'', the current one. Everything a note describes is implemented
unless the note says otherwise: a note about work still to come says so in its
status — ''deferred'' (decided, not done), ''proposed'' (not decided) — and names
what is missing rather than a version number. A ''record'' is a note kept for
what it explains about a past design, not for what the code does today.

!! Non-goals

* ''No remote protocol.'' The daemon is local-only and makes no network call ([[No network access]]). Working across machines is done around it: a repository's files may sit on a mounted remote filesystem as long as its store is kept local ([[Network and special filesystems]]), and synchronising two repositories needs both loaded in the same daemon.
* ''No user accounts, no access control.'' A single local user is assumed. The session token is not a user-to-user mechanism: it keeps the processes that cannot read it — web pages in your browser first of all — away from the loopback ports ([[Session tokens]], [[Why a session token]]).
* ''No two daemons on one repository.'' There is no conflict resolution between daemon instances: a repository's store is locked by the daemon that loaded it, and a second load is refused at once ([[The repository lock]]).

Portability is a separate matter: Linux is the platform metafolder runs on, macOS
and Windows are design targets ([[Platform]]).

!! Why say so

Each of these is a door deliberately left closed, not an oversight: a change
that needs one of them is a change of scope, to be decided as such. They also
bound the threat model — single user, loopback — that [[Security]] starts from.
